{
  "index_test": "whole-animal-in-vivo",
  "index_test_evidence": "The zebrafish model organism is increasingly used for assessing drug toxicity and safety and numerous studies confirm that mammalian and zebrafish toxicity profiles are strikingly similar.",
  "decision": "include",
  "reason": "The study uses whole zebrafish (a living non-human animal) to predict drug-induced toxicity in humans. It reports on the similarity of toxicity profiles between zebrafish and mammals and provides specific examples of concordance, such as cardiotoxicity and developmental toxicity, including quantitative data like \"One hundred small molecules were recently tested and results showed that 22 of 23 drugs that cause QT prolongation in humans consistently caused bradycardia and blocked AV conduction in zebrafish\" and \"The assay was ranked good (>70% <80%) for specificity and excellent (>80%) for sensitivity. Furthermore, this assay format presented a 75% success rate in identifying nonteratogenic compounds and a 100% success rate in identifying teratogens\". This meets the inclusion criteria for reporting quantitative measures of agreement between animal and human results."
}
